I'm a customer engineer at Google Cloud  today I'm going to talk about VPC design  patterns that Google Cloud customers can  leverage one of the early design  decisions customers make on Google cloud  is related to the VPC Network  customers can have unique set of network  requirements but I will cover a few of  the common VPC design patterns with that  in mind let's get started a quick  refresher on VPC Concepts  vpcs are Global which means vpcs can  span across multiple regions subnets are  Regional and we have a shared VPC that  can be shared across multiple projects  within your Google Cloud organization  shared VPC is a core component of our  today's discussion together these  features of Google Cloud VPC helps  customers design simple yet secure  architectures  now let's review what is a shared VPC at  a high level you have one host project  and multiple service projects host  project is where you would create the  VPC and the subnets service projects are  then attached to the host project you  can share a subnet with one or multiple  service projects IIM users in a service  project with select IIM roles such as  compute admin project owner among others  will be given Network user permissions  to the shared subnets in the host  project this allows the IM users in the  service project to create resources in  the shared subnet a shared VPC allows  customers to centrally manage firewall  rules submit Creations connections  between on-prem and VPC rotting rules  among many other network operations  let's start with a simple design let's  create two separate networks one for  production and one for non-production  both of which can have subnets in  multiple regions these subnets are then  shared with multiple service projects  where applications will eventually be  hosted as a best practice we recommend  customers to create multiple service  projects for production and  non-production workloads naturally you  will take the development version of  your application and deploy it in the  non-production supplement and the  production version of your application  in the production subnet this design  gives you isolation between production  and non-production networks while giving  your network teams the ability to manage  network operations such as Fireball rule  management routing tables using single  Google Cloud project  how about connectivity to on-prem or  other clouds for that we can leverage  Google managed hivpn or Cloud  interconnect a VPN provides encrypted  ipsec VPN allows all the internet Cloud  interconnect offers private connectivity  between customers Data Center and Google  Cloud VPC when designed for high  availability your architecture would  look something like this you would have  two VPN tunnels or two interconnect  vlans per region from on-prem to each  one of these vpcs  however some customers like to  consolidate their VPN connections or  Cloud interconnect view lines and be  able to share them with more than just  one VPC especially when the Nintendo  scale Beyond just two vpcs so how do we  do that Hub and spoke model  building on what we already have let's  add a hub VPC to our core Network  project The Hub VPC can be connected to  the spokes via VPC peering or Google  managed hivpn how about the connectivity  from on-prem how many connections do we  need now if you recollect in our  previous design we had four connections  to connect two vpcs  since we now have a hub VPC we can share  the on-prem connectivity to the spokes  via the Hub VPC  some customers might want to create  network segregation between certain  workloads which means they might create  additional spoke vpcs for connectivity  to on-prem though all the spoke vpcs  will share the existing connection via  the Hub VPC Hub and spoke design lets  customers consolidate their hybrid  connections while allowing them to scale  spoke vpcs  some customers have common tools such as  active directory centralized logging  systems development tools that are used  across production and non-production  subnets these tools need to be accessed  from both production and non-production  vpcs so where do we deploy these tools  let's take a look  one way to architect this is to share a  subnet from The Hub VPC with a shared  services project tools deployed in this  project can be accessed from all the  spoke vpcs and the on-prem network  let's talk about Google managed services  such as Cloud SQL memory store among  others these Services leverage VPC  peering to provide private IP  connectivity this model is referred to  as private service access but VPC  peering is non-transitive so how does  this affect our design let's take a look  consider this use case in a hub and  spoke model what if direct access is  required from on-prem to Cloud SQL or  other Google managed services that use  private service access since these  services use VPC peeling to connect to  the respective shared VPC if there was  another VPC bearing between the Hub and  the spokes it would make the connection  transitive so on-prem would not be able  to reach these services  how do we design for this use case  one pattern is to use Google managed  hivpn between the Hub and this box this  will allow the on-prem systems to reach  Google managed services that use private  service access via the Hub VPC  with that our design would look  something like this  now we can extend the same design to  support multi-region deployments  in the case of multi-region deployments  typically customers have data center in  multiple regions  so they would create multiple Cloud  interconnect vlans or havpn connections  to the respective Google Cloud region  put it all together this is how a  multi-region hub and spoke VPC design  could look like  thank you for watching
 

